// In-depth battles, batch r: tondibi, hansan, myeongnyang, sekigahara, sarhu, breitenfeld.
// Checked against the English Wikipedia articles on each battle and on the Moroccan invasion of the Songhai Empire,
// the Joseon naval campaigns of 1592, Yi Sun-sin, and the wider wars (Thirty Years' War, Imjin War, Ming-Jin war).

Object.assign(GLOSSARY, {
  "ásquia": "The title of the emperors of Songhai. The name comes from Askia Muhammad, who took the throne in the late 15th century and gave the title to his dynasty.",
  "ulemá": "The scholars and judges of a Muslim city, specialists in religion and law. In Timbuktu, they were one of the city's elites.",
  "panokseon": "The main Korean warship of the time: tall, wide, with a strong, flat-bottomed hull. It stood up well to the recoil of its own cannons, and so could carry many of them.",
  "navio tartaruga": "The Korean geobukseon: a ship with its deck covered by a roof, to protect the rowers and soldiers from enemy arrows and bullets. It sailed into the middle of the enemy fleet and fired in all directions.",
  "asa de garça": "The U-shaped naval formation (in Korean, hagikjin): the fleet spreads its two ends like the wings of a bird, lets the enemy in the middle and fires on it from three sides at once.",
  "atakebune": "The great Japanese warship of the time, tall and heavy, built for boarding: soldiers with swords and arquebuses leapt onto the enemy's deck.",
  "koku": "A measure of rice used in Japan to calculate the wealth of a piece of land. One koku was the rice an adult ate in a year. A domain 'of 520,000 koku' was enormous.",
  "ronin": "A samurai without a lord, who has lost his master or clan and lives by fighting for whoever pays. Many were recruited in a hurry before Sekigahara.",
  "sutegamari": "The samurai 'sacrificial retreat': small groups stay behind to hold off the enemy to the death, while the lord escapes.",
  "jin posterior": "The state that Nurhaci founded in 1616 in northeast Asia, ruled by the Jurchens. In 1636, it became the Qing dynasty.",
  "jurchens": "The people of the forests and mountains of northeast Asia, from whom Nurhaci came. They only adopted the name 'Manchu' in 1635.",
  "oito estandartes": "The organization of Nurhaci's army: all men and their families belonged to one of eight groups, each with its own colored banner. It was at once army, registry office and community.",
  "tércio": "The great Spanish and imperial infantry block of the 16th and 17th centuries, thousands of men strong, a square of pikemen with musketeers in the corners. Strong from the front, but slow to turn.",
  "brigada": "In the Swedish army, a group of three or four battalions working together. It was smaller and easier to maneuver than a tercio.",
  "artilharia de regimento": "Light cannons, with small crews, that moved along with each Swedish infantry regiment and gave it direct support, instead of sitting in a large, stationary group.",
  "couraceiros": "Horsemen in breastplate and helmet, armed with pistol and sword. In the Thirty Years' War, they delivered the shock against the enemy infantry.",
  "salva": "Fire from many musketeers at the same time, on command. A well-executed volley knocks down the front ranks of those advancing.",
  "conselho dos cinco anciãos": "The group of five great lords that Hideyoshi appointed, near death, to rule Japan until his son grew up. Tokugawa Ieyasu was the most powerful of them."
});

Object.assign(DEEP, {
  tondibi: {
    war: "Moroccan invasion of the Songhai Empire (1590–1599)",
    hook: "A two-hour battle in which gunpowder brought down the greatest empire of West Africa.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "For more than a hundred years, the Songhai Empire dominated West Africa, from the headwaters of the Senegal River to what is now Niger. Ruled by the dynasty of the [[askias|ásquia]], it controlled the gold and salt routes, and its cities, Gao and Timbuktu, were centers of trade and learning. The Niger River was the empire's highway.",
        "On the other side of the Sahara, Morocco was living the height of the Saadian dynasty. Sultan Ahmad al-Mansur had come to power after the battle of Alcácer-Quibir, in 1578, when Morocco defeated Portugal and killed King Sebastian. He reorganized the army along Ottoman lines, with firearms and artillery, and the cost of these defenses left the treasury almost empty. Mansur believed Songhai had gold mines that explained its wealth, and he wanted that gold.",
        { h: "The road to Tondibi" },
        { timeline: [
          ["1578", "Alcácer-Quibir: Morocco beats Portugal. Mansur takes the throne."],
          ["1582–1583", "The askia Daud dies. His sons and brothers fight over the succession and weaken Songhai."],
          ["1589–1590", "Mansur demands gold in exchange for the salt taken from the Taghaza mines. The askia Ishaq II refuses."],
          ["16 Oct. 1590", "Judar Pasha leaves Marrakesh with the army."],
          ["28 Feb. 1591", "After four months of marching across the Sahara, he reaches the Niger River."],
          ["13 Mar. 1591", "Battle of Tondibi, north of Gao."]
        ]},
        "According to the sources, Mansur's advisers warned him that attacking another Muslim country was illegal. He set the warnings aside. The Taghaza salt mines were looted and razed on the way, and the army marched on to Gao."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "The main players", blocks: [
        { h: "Ahmad al-Mansur, sultan of Morocco" },
        "He became known as 'the Golden' (al-Dhahabi), partly because of the gold that Sudan sent after the conquest. He ruled for 24 years, took the title of caliph and wanted to be the leader of the Muslim world. He tried to expand east, against the Ottomans, and failed. It was to the south that his ambition paid off, at least for a while.",
        { h: "Judar Pasha" },
        "He was a [[pasha|paxá]] of Spanish origin, a eunuch captured as a child. Mansur sent him to command the expedition. It was he who organized the crossing of the desert, and it was he who, after the victory, was disappointed by what he found: little gold, and empty cities.",
        { h: "Askia Ishaq II" },
        "He ruled an empire weakened by succession quarrels. He was the one who refused to pay Mansur. After Tondibi, he offered peace in exchange for tribute, but it was too late. He died in 1591, after his brother Muhammad Gao declared himself askia."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The crossing" },
        "In October 1590, Judar set out from Marrakesh with an enormous convoy: 8,000 camels, 1,000 pack horses, 1,000 grooms, 600 workers and eight cannons. The walk across the Sahara took four months. The sources give no exact numbers of deaths on the way, they only say it was hard. On 28 February 1591, the army reached the Niger.",
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The Moroccans were few. The numbers vary: one source speaks of about 4,000 men, another of 20,000, counting the whole expeditionary army. In the battle, there were some 1,500 light horsemen and some 2,000 to 2,500 infantry, most with [[arquebuses|arcabuz]], plus six to eight cannons. Half the infantry were converts to Islam (many of European origin), and the other half descended from Muslims who left the old emirate of Granada, in Spain.",
        "The Songhai were far more numerous, but nobody knows how many. The Tarikh al-Fattash chronicle speaks of 18,000 horsemen and 9,700 foot soldiers. Other estimates range from 12,500 horsemen and 30,000 infantry up to 80,000 men in total. All agree that the Songhai were more. What they did not have was firearms: their strength was cavalry.",
        { note: "Even the date is debated. Most modern works give 13 March 1591, but the Tarikh al-Sudan chronicle points to 12 April." },
        { h: "The place" },
        "The sources say little about the terrain. We know the encounter was at Tondibi (Tankondibogho), a little north of Gao, on the bank of the Niger.",
        { h: "The battle" },
        "The Songhai plan was simple and, for people who had never seen gunpowder, reasonable. A thousand oxen would be loosed against the Moroccan line, to break the formation and hide the infantry coming behind. Then the cavalry would come.",
        "The Moroccans waited. When the oxen arrived, the noise of the arquebuses and cannons frightened them, and the herd turned around and stampeded into the Songhai ranks.",
        "The Songhai infantry advanced anyway, and was thrown back by the arquebuses. Then came the cavalry. There was a short clash, and Judar set his arquebusiers in position and opened fire, together with the cannons. The surviving horsemen fled or were cut down, and the rear guard fought hand to hand until it was destroyed.",
        "It all lasted about two hours. According to the Tarikh al-Sudan chronicle, some Songhai soldiers, instead of fleeing, sat down on their shields and waited for death."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "The sources give no death toll for either side. What is known is the rest: the Songhai army fell apart and abandoned Gao, and Judar occupied Gao, Timbuktu and Djenné. Since the cities had already been emptied, the booty was small, well below what Mansur expected.",
        { h: "What came next" },
        "Judar went so far as to forward to Mansur a peace offer from Ishaq II. The sources differ on the terms: one speaks of 100,000 pieces of gold and 1,000 slaves in exchange for the Moroccan withdrawal. Mansur was furious, because he expected Judar to seize the gold mines. He sent another commander, Mahmud ibn Zarqun, who arrived in August 1591 and demoted Judar to second in command.",
        "The conquest, however, never really finished. The Moroccans were few, the empire was enormous, communications were poor and the climate of the Niger, hot and full of mosquitoes, made the troops sick. In Timbuktu, there was a revolt against Mahmud's harsh rule, and in April 1594 he deported the city's [[ulama|ulemá]] to Marrakesh, among them Ahmad Baba, one of the most famous scholars of the region. Songhai resistance began to use firearms and to fight as guerrillas. The heirs to the throne went to Dendi, in the southeast, where the askias carried on for about two and a half centuries.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "The Songhai Empire disappeared as a regional power, and the region broke up into dozens of smaller kingdoms. Mansur died in 1603, Morocco went into civil war, and in 1618 it stopped ruling Sudan directly. The region became the Pashalik of Timbuktu, ruled by the descendants of the Moroccan soldiers, the Arma, and only nominally tied to Morocco, until the early 19th century. The caravans kept carrying gold, slaves and ivory north, but Saharan trade lost strength, partly because the Europeans diverted gold and slaves to the Atlantic.",
        "Tondibi is often remembered as an example of how a difference in technology, here the [[arquebus|arcabuz]] against a cavalry with no firearms, can decide a battle in which the side with the technology is the smaller.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The victory was quick, but the conquest was costly. The gold Mansur expected never came in the quantity he dreamed of, and the gold of the Americas, which Spain brought to Europe, began to compete with that of Sudan." },
        { note: "The numbers for the Songhai vary from some 10,000 to 80,000, depending on the source. All agree that they outnumbered the Moroccans." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  hansan: {
    war: "Imjin War: the Japanese invasion of Korea (1592–1598)",
    hook: "A decoy, a wing-shaped formation and a Japanese fleet almost entirely on the sea floor.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "In 1592, Japan had just come out of more than a century of civil war. The lord who unified it, Toyotomi Hideyoshi, had an enormous army, full of veterans and of [[arquebuses|arcabuz]], and a bold idea: to conquer China, passing through Korea. Korea, ruled by the Joseon dynasty, had lived in peace for a long time and was unprepared.",
        "The invasion began at Busan, in the south, and within a few weeks the Japanese were advancing north. On land, almost nothing stopped them. The king fled Seoul, and the Japanese army reached Pyongyang, farther north. But an army that advances far needs food and ammunition, and for that Japan counted on its ships. The sea was the weak point.",
        { h: "The road to Hansan" },
        { timeline: [
          ["1591", "Yi Sun-sin takes the naval command of the left Jeolla region, at Yeosu, a year before the war."],
          ["May–June 1592", "Yi's first victories, at Okpo, Sacheon, Dangpo and Danghangpo, with almost no Korean losses."],
          ["Mid-1592", "The Japanese army reaches Pyongyang. The Korean court flees to Uiju. Hideyoshi sends about 100 ships, including the heaviest, to reinforce the army and destroy Yi's fleet."],
          ["10–12 August", "Yi Eok-gi joins Yi Sun-sin at Yeosu. On the 12th, the fleet joins Won Gyun, at Noryang, and the Korean squadron reaches 56 warships (other sources say more)."],
          ["14 August", "Battle of Hansan. On the same day, Yi also attacks Angolpo."]
        ]},
        "Hideyoshi's reinforcements were the great test of the war. If the Japanese fleet won, the army at Pyongyang would get food and ammunition, and Korea would be lost. If it lost, the Korean fleet would be master of the coast."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "The main players", blocks: [
        { h: "Yi Sun-sin, 47 years old" },
        "A career officer, he had been appointed to the naval command of the left Jeolla region in March 1591, a year before the war. In 1592, his first victories, with almost no losses, gave the Korean navy the confidence it lacked. At Hansan, he showed what set him apart: choosing the place and the moment of the fight, instead of accepting what the enemy offered.",
        { h: "Wakisaka Yasuharu, Japanese lord" },
        "He commanded the fleet of 73 ships that entered the Gyeonnaeryang strait. He was a Japanese lord, and his fleet entered the strait alone. The other two commanders of the expedition, Kuki Yoshitaka and Kato Yoshiaki, were at Angolpo with another 42 ships. Wakisaka reappears in the following war and, later, at Sekigahara.",
        { h: "Yi Eok-gi and Won Gyun" },
        "They were two other Korean admirals. Yi Eok-gi commanded the right Jeolla region. Won Gyun, of the right Gyeongsang region, joined Yi's fleet on 12 August. Later, he would take Yi's place and lose almost the whole fleet, which would lead to Myeongnyang."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The Koreans had 56 warships, among them at least three [[turtle ships|navio tartaruga]], according to the most used count. Other sources speak of up to 70 ships, counting support vessels. The large Korean ships, the [[panokseon]], were tall and strong and carried many cannons.",
        "The Japanese had 73 ships: 36 large, 24 medium and 13 small (other sources say 82). The large ones, the [[atakebune]], were built for boarding: the Japanese plan was to come alongside the enemy ship and cross to the deck, where the samurai were unbeatable. The Koreans knew this, and all of Yi's plan came down to preventing it: fighting from afar, with the cannons.",
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "The Gyeonnaeryang strait runs between the island of Geoje and the mainland. According to the accounts, it is narrow and has reefs, and the large Korean ships would have no room to maneuver there. The open water in front of Hansan island gave the fleet room to move and form its line.",
        { h: "The battle" },
        { formation: true },
        "On 13 August, the Korean fleet, assembled, left Miruk island. On the morning of the 14th, it met a Japanese scout ship and chased it. The chase was interrupted when the Koreans sighted a large Japanese fleet in the strait. It was Wakisaka's.",
        "Yi sent a small part of the fleet, a few ships, as a decoy. The Japanese took the bait: they came out of the strait after them and headed for the open water in front of Hansan island. Instead of a fleet in formation, they were ships chasing, little by little, the Koreans who pretended to flee.",
        "When the enemy fleet was in open sea, Yi gave the order. The decoy ships turned, and the rest of the fleet opened into a U: the [[crane wing|asa de garça]] formation. The larger ships, with the turtle ships, stayed in the center, and the lighter ones on the tips.",
        "The Japanese entered the U. From then on, the Korean cannons fired from three sides, at a distance. The Japanese could not come alongside to board, and those coming behind, squeezed by the ones in front, had no way to maneuver. The fighting lasted a good part of the day, and most of the fleet was destroyed.",
        "Fourteen Japanese ships that never joined the fight escaped, and Wakisaka withdrew to Gimhae.",
        { h: "On the same day, at Angolpo" },
        "News of the defeat reached Busan within hours. Kuki and Kato, who were on their way, went with their 42 ships to Angolpo, near the coast, where they hoped to fight on more favorable ground. Yi went after them on the same day. The Japanese stayed on the beach, and did not follow him out to open sea. The Korean fleet bombarded the anchored ships for hours, until the Japanese fell back inland. Kuki and Kato survived.",
        { h: "The soldiers on the island" },
        "Japanese soldiers were left stranded on a small island. Won Gyun was put in charge of clearing it, but withdrew after receiving a false warning that a large Japanese fleet was approaching. Those on the island fled on rafts made of wreckage."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "The Japanese lost 59 of the 73 ships, according to the most common count: 47 sunk or burned and 12 captured. The 14 that remained fled. If we count Angolpo, the total passes 100 ships. On the Korean side, losses were light: 19 dead and 114 wounded, and no ship lost.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "On 23 August, Hideyoshi sent Todo Takatora to reinforce operations in Korea and suspended naval operations at Busan. Without control of the sea, the Japanese army lost the supply route it wanted, and the invasion lost strength. Yi took Hansan island as a base and began to plan an attack on the great port of Busan.",
        "The American historian Homer Hulbert compared Hansan to the battle of Salamis, and said it was the death sentence of Hideyoshi's invasion. The British naval historian George Ballard ranked Yi's campaign as a series of successes without equal in maritime history.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The date differs by source: 14 or 15 August 1592. By the Korean lunar calendar, it was the eighth day of the seventh month, which equals 14 August in the Gregorian." },
        { note: "The numbers vary. One source speaks of 56 Korean ships against 73 Japanese, another of 70 against 82. In one of the versions, Yi sent six ships as a decoy, and 63 enemy ships followed them." },
        { note: "Hansan and Angolpo happened on the same day. That is why the totals of Japanese ships lost vary: counting only Hansan, it is 59 of 73; adding Angolpo, it passes 100." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  myeongnyang: {
    war: "Imjin War: the second Japanese invasion of Korea (1597–1598)",
    hook: "Thirteen ships against more than a hundred, and the sea did half the work.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "After the first invasion, Japan and China negotiated peace, and the negotiations failed. In 1597, Hideyoshi sent a second army to Korea. But Korea had a serious problem: intrigue at court.",
        "Admiral Yi Sun-sin, the hero of the first war, refused an order from General Kim Gyeong-seo to ambush a Japanese fleet, because the place had hidden rocks and he distrusted the source. Enemies at court took advantage: Yi was accused, replaced by Won Gyun and imprisoned. He was tortured, nearly executed and demoted to common soldier.",
        { h: "The road to Myeongnyang" },
        { timeline: [
          ["Early 1597", "Japan invades Korea for the second time."],
          ["1597", "Yi is imprisoned, tortured and demoted. Won Gyun takes command of the fleet."],
          ["Summer 1597", "At Chilcheollyang, the Japanese fleet, under Todo Takatora, almost destroys the Korean navy. Won Gyun dies."],
          ["Soon after", "Yi is hurriedly restored to command. He finds only 13 warships."],
          ["26 October 1597", "Battle of Myeongnyang."],
          ["23 November", "The Japanese burn the village of Asan, where Yi's family home stood. His youngest son dies."]
        ]},
        "The disaster of Chilcheollyang was total. About thirteen warships were left, saved by Pae Seol (who would later flee and be executed), by Kim Eok-chu and by one more commander. The Japanese army, advancing on Seoul, needed reinforcements by sea, and for that the Japanese fleet had to enter the Yellow Sea. The only obstacle was Yi."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "The main players", blocks: [
        { h: "Yi Sun-sin, 52 years old" },
        "Yi had been hurriedly returned to command. King Seonjo, faced with the ruined fleet, ordered him to abandon the ships and join General Gwon Yul's land troops. Yi answered that he still had twelve warships, and that, as long as he lived, the enemy would not dare to despise the navy. This answer is one of the most famous in the history of Korea.",
        { h: "Kurushima Michifusa and Todo Takatora" },
        "Kurushima Michifusa commanded the Japanese vanguard. Todo Takatora, another Japanese commander, had led the victory at Chilcheollyang. He survived Myeongnyang, but was wounded and nearly drowned. Three years later, at Sekigahara, he fought in the East, beside Ieyasu.",
        { h: "The Korean captains" },
        "The captains, such as An Wi and Kim Ung-ham, came from the disaster of Chilcheollyang and hesitated at first. They only joined the fight after seeing Yi's flagship alone in front."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The Koreans had 13 warships, plus 32 small scout ships that, according to Yi's own report, probably did not fight. The number of Yi's ships appears as 12 or 13, depending on the source: his answer to the king says twelve, and the counts of the disaster say thirteen.",
        "The Japanese, according to Korean sources, had 120 to 133 warships, and some 200 support vessels: in total, up to some 330. They were at least ten times as many.",
        { h: "The strait" },
        "The Myeongnyang strait, near Jindo island, is very narrow and has a current of about 10 knots, which changes direction roughly every three hours. Yi knew this, and chose the place because of it. He did not want to fight in the south, where the current favored the attacker, but a little to the north, where the water was calmer. The strait was too narrow for the Japanese fleet to pass all at once: it could only attack in small groups, with no room to surround.",
        { h: "The battle" },
        { formation: true },
        "On the morning of 26 October, the scouts warned that the Japanese fleet was gathering in a small bay, at the southern entrance of the strait. Yi took his fleet out of Usuyeong and went to block the northern exit, where he anchored.",
        "The Japanese entered. Kurushima led, and Yi's flagship advanced to face the vanguard. For quite a while, Yi's ship fought alone. The others, shaken by the earlier disaster, stayed behind. Yi's diary says that Kim Eok-chu's ship stayed a short distance away.",
        "The resistance of Yi's ship ended up encouraging the others. The first to arrive was An Wi, then Kim Ung-ham, and then the rest of the fleet.",
        "In the strait, the Japanese fleet bunched up. The ships in front could not back off, and those behind pushed. Then the tide turned. The current began to push the Japanese back, the ships collided, and the fire of the Korean cannons hit clusters of motionless ships. Yi ordered an advance and, according to his report, 31 ships were rammed or destroyed. The strong current drowned many people.",
        "Todo's memoirs say that Kurushima was killed early in the fight, and that Todo himself ended up in the water and was saved because his men pulled him out. Wounded, he withdrew.",
        "By nightfall, the Japanese were no longer coming. But the tide, the wind and the difference in numbers made it risky to continue, and Yi pulled back to Dangsa island."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "The Japanese lost about 30 ships, according to the most cited counts (31 by Yi's report). On the Korean side, no ship was lost. Yi's ship had at least two dead and three wounded, and at least eight men drowned on An Wi's ship. A Japanese prisoner said half the soldiers were killed or wounded. The Todo family says that half of its elders died or were wounded.",
        "Even so, Yi did not stay. With a much smaller force, he pulled back to resupply. The Japanese made a small raid along the west coast and went back to consolidating the south coast.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "Myeongnyang blocked the Japanese fleet and kept it from reaching the Yellow Sea, from where it could supply the army marching on Seoul. The survivors of Chilcheollyang and the refugee ships joined Yi again, and in early 1598 the Chinese navy began to fight at his side. Yi died at Noryang, on 16 December 1598, struck by a stray bullet, in the last great combat of the war.",
        "It is considered one of the most improbable naval victories in history.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The iron chain: some accounts say Yi stretched iron chains across the strait to trap the Japanese ships. Yi's diary does not mention this." },
        { note: "The strategic victory: a Japanese source argues the battle was in fact a strategic victory for Japan, because the Korean navy abandoned the strait afterward. But Japanese historians of the colonial period and the Imperial Navy, like most Japanese historians today, consider it a decisive Korean victory." },
        { note: "The eve phrase: a famous saying is attributed to Yi, something like 'whoever accepts death, lives; whoever wants to live, dies', said on the eve of the battle. It appears in many popular accounts, but the most cited text on the battle carries another: the one he told the king." },
        { note: "The numbers: some Korean sources count as lost ships that were only damaged, and that is why the Japanese loss counts vary." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  sekigahara: {
    war: "The struggle for power after Hideyoshi (1600)",
    hook: "On paper, the West won. But the battle was decided by those who did not fight, and by those who switched sides.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "Japan in the 16th century was being reunified, after more than a century of civil war, by Oda Nobunaga and then by Toyotomi Hideyoshi. Hideyoshi died in 1598, leaving an heir, Hideyori, only five years old. Before dying, he appointed a [[council of five great lords|conselho dos cinco anciãos]] to rule until the boy grew up. The most powerful of them was Tokugawa Ieyasu.",
        "The country was divided. Ishida Mitsunari, an administrator loyal to the Toyotomi family, had enemies among the generals who came back from the Korean war, such as Kato Kiyomasa, who blamed him for their troubles in the campaign. In 1599, Maeda Toshiie died, another of the five, who kept the balance. Ieyasu took advantage, and drew several generals, among them Kato and Fukushima Masanori, to his side.",
        { h: "The road to Sekigahara" },
        { timeline: [
          ["1598", "Hideyoshi dies. The heir, Hideyori, is five."],
          ["1599", "Maeda Toshiie dies. Ieyasu grows in power."],
          ["Summer 1600", "Uesugi Kagekatsu raises an army in the north. Ieyasu questions him, and Uesugi's adviser answers with a mocking letter. Ieyasu takes his main army north."],
          ["Summer 1600", "With Ieyasu away, Mitsunari and Otani Yoshitsugu raise the Western coalition, with Mori Terumoto as commander. Terumoto takes Osaka castle."],
          ["Summer 1600", "The East takes Gifu castle. Mitsunari decides to fight in the open, at Sekigahara."],
          ["21 October 1600", "Battle of Sekigahara."]
        ]},
        "Mitsunari planned to hold the castles of Gifu and Ogaki to delay the East. But Fukushima Masanori and Ii Naomasa took Gifu before the reinforcements arrived, and Mitsunari concluded that Ieyasu was heading straight for Osaka. He decided to face him on the way, at Sekigahara.",
        "On the eve, a secret agreement changed everything: Kikkawa Hiroie, a vassal of the Mori, arranged with the East, through Kuroda Yoshitaka and his son Kuroda Nagamasa, that the Mori would switch sides in exchange for a pardon."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "The main players", blocks: [
        { h: "Tokugawa Ieyasu, 57 years old" },
        "A veteran of many wars and the most powerful of the five elders. By taking the army north against the Uesugi, he left the west without him, and it was in that absence that Mitsunari raised the coalition. Ieyasu came back and went after him.",
        { h: "Ishida Mitsunari, 40 years old" },
        "He was not a warrior, he was an administrator, loyal to the Toyotomi family. He was not well liked: many lords resented him, especially because of the Korean war. He put together a coalition that looked solid on paper, but, according to historians such as Watanabe Daimon and Yuichi Goza, he overestimated the authority of the Toyotomi name. Otani Yoshitsugu, one of his main allies, warned him against the battle.",
        { h: "Kobayakawa Hideaki, 19 years old" },
        "The young lord who stayed with about 15,000 men on Mount Matsuo, high up, watching the battlefield. His role is the most debated of all.",
        { h: "The other names" },
        "On Ieyasu's side, Ii Naomasa and his about 3,600 'red devils', famous for their red armor; Fukushima Masanori; Kuroda Nagamasa; Honda Tadakatsu; Todo Takatora; Hosokawa Tadaoki. On the Western side, Ukita Hideie, Otani Yoshitsugu, Shima Sakon, Shimazu Yoshihiro, Chosokabe Morichika and Konishi Yukinaga. Mori Terumoto was the official chief, but stayed in Osaka."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The numbers are very uncertain. The sources cite values such as 75,000, 81,890, 88,888 and 120,000, and it is not easy to say what each one counts (the armies on paper or those actually on the field). In general, the two sides were similar in size, and the West is usually described as the more numerous. Who was not there, on either side, was an important part of the East: Tokugawa Hidetada, Ieyasu's son, with about 38,000 men, was stuck in the siege of Ueda castle, against the Sanada, and did not arrive in time.",
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "A narrow basin between Mount Nangu and Mount Matsuo. The tight shape forced the West to line up in a single row, which limited the advantage of having more men. Kobayakawa's men were on Mount Matsuo, and Otani on the Fujikawa plateau. The East placed itself in front of the West.",
        { h: "The fog" },
        "On the morning of 21 October, a dense mist, after days of rain, made the vanguards of both sides bump into each other at dawn. The two armies pulled back, formed up and began to fight. By tradition, the battle began around 8 o'clock. The researcher Ando Yuichiro suggests about 10 o'clock.",
        { h: "The battle in six movements" },
        { formation: true },
        "First, Ii Naomasa advanced with his about 3,600 men against the center of the West. Fukushima Masanori followed and attacked Ukita Hideie. For a while, the fight was stalled, with a lot of arquebus fire and many arrows. Ukita, weakened by an earlier internal revolt in his clan and with many [[ronin]] recruited in a hurry, gave ground to Fukushima's better-trained soldiers.",
        "Second, Kuroda Nagamasa's men made a [[flanking|flanquear]] movement along the side. On the other side, the 360-man vanguard of Hiratsuka Tamehiro, of Otani, was defeated by Wakisaka, Todo and Kyogoku.",
        "Third, the most famous point. Kobayakawa stayed on Mount Matsuo without moving, watching. The traditional version says he stayed neutral until around midday and then attacked Otani's flank. There is a theory that says he had already switched sides before the battle, judging by letters exchanged with Kuroda Nagamasa. When Kobayakawa came down, others also switched sides: Wakisaka Yasuharu, Ogawa Suketada, Akaza Naoyasu and Kutsuki Mototsuna. Several of them had already made deals with Todo Takatora days earlier.",
        "Fourth, Otani was crushed. He killed himself, and his men fell back. Toda Katsushige died in the confusion.",
        "Fifth, the Mori. Mori Terumoto stayed in Osaka, and the army of Mori Hidemoto did not move. Kikkawa Hiroie sent a message to Ieyasu and placed his troops in front of Hidemoto, to keep him from advancing. He also blocked Chosokabe. Some of the Mori men fought, but the great army that looked like a second force of the West stayed largely still.",
        "Sixth, the rout. Mitsunari ordered a retreat when the right flank gave way. Shima Sakon was shot by an arquebus, mortally wounded by Kuroda's flank. Shimazu Yoshihiro was surrounded and attacked from behind by Kobayakawa's men. He cut his way through the middle of the enemy with about 200 men alive. Ii Naomasa chased him until he was wounded by a shot. This retreat is called [[sutegamari]], and tradition says Shimazu Toyohisa made a heroic charge in the rear, although modern historians suspect the story was embellished.",
        "The letter Ieyasu himself wrote on the day of the battle suggests the fight lasted about two hours, between 10 o'clock and midday."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "The losses are very uncertain. The Sekigahara Gunki Taisei gives 8,000 to 32,000 dead in one list and 4,000 to 10,000 in another. The Tokugawa chronicles speak of 35,270 heads collected. It is hard to trust these counts.",
        "On 6 November, Ishida Mitsunari, Konishi Yukinaga and Ankokuji Ekei, captured, were executed. About 6.8 million [[koku]] of lands were redistributed, most of them to Ieyasu's allies. Kobayakawa received a domain of 520,000 koku, but the grant was only verbal. The Mori lands were reduced to Suo and Nagato. About 87 [[daimyo|daimiô]] of the West lost their domains. The Shimazu were the only Western clan that managed to negotiate and keep their lands.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "In 1603, Ieyasu was named [[shogun|xogum]] and founded the Tokugawa [[shogunate|xogunato]], which ruled Japan in peace until 1868, for more than 250 years. Sekigahara was, in practice, the beginning of an era. Hideyori, the Toyotomi heir, still survived until the siege of Osaka, in 1614–1615, when Ieyasu put an end to the family.",
        "The domains of Choshu and Satsuma, heirs of the Mori and Shimazu clans, punished in 1600, were among those that overthrew the shogunate in 1868. The revenge of the defeated took more than two centuries.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The German general Jakob Meckel, adviser to the Japanese army in the Meiji period, studied the map and concluded that the West, with more men and on higher ground, must have won. He only accepted the result when a Japanese officer explained what really happened." },
        { note: "Ieyasu's shot: the best-known story says Ieyasu ordered shots fired in the direction of Kobayakawa to force him to choose. Stephen Turnbull, Masayuki Fujimoto and Yuichi Goza doubt it, because the distance was probably too great and the story only appears in Edo period sources." },
        { note: "The hour: by the Japanese calendar, it was the 15th day of the 9th month of the fifth year of Keicho. Tradition gives 8 o'clock for the start, and recent research proposes 10 o'clock." },
        { note: "A detail: Wakisaka Yasuharu, who switched sides at Sekigahara, is the same man who had commanded the Japanese fleet defeated at Hansan. And Todo Takatora, who won at Chilcheollyang and nearly drowned at Myeongnyang, was also there, on the winner's side." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  sarhu: {
    war: "War between the Later Jin and the Ming (1618–1644)",
    hook: "Four armies came by four roads. Nurhaci faced them one at a time.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "At the start of the 17th century, China of the [[Ming|ming]] dynasty was still an enormous empire. To the northeast, in the mountains and forests of Manchuria, lived a people of hunters and farmers, the [[Jurchens|jurchens]], divided into tribes.",
        "One of their chiefs, Nurhaci, spent decades uniting these tribes. He organized all the men into a system called the [[Eight Banners|oito estandartes]], which was at once army and administration. In 1616, he proclaimed himself khan of the [[Later Jin|jin posterior]]. Only the Yehe, a neighboring tribe, still resisted.",
        { h: "The road to Sarhu" },
        { timeline: [
          ["1616", "Nurhaci proclaims himself khan of the Later Jin."],
          ["1618", "He declares the 'Seven Grievances' against the Ming, takes Fushun, Qinghe and other cities, and withdraws."],
          ["Early 1619", "He attacks the Yehe, to provoke the Ming."],
          ["March–April 1619", "The Ming send Yang Hao, who divides his army into four columns, all heading for Nurhaci's capital, Hetu Ala."],
          ["14 to 18 April 1619", "Battle of Sarhu."]
        ]},
        "The Ming plan was simple: four columns, coming from four sides, would converge on Hetu Ala and crush Nurhaci. Korea, which had been helped by the Ming army in the war against Japan, 27 years earlier, sent a force of musketeers. The plan had one problem: the columns came by separate roads, in mountain country, and could not support one another."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "The main players", blocks: [
        { h: "Nurhaci, about 60 years old" },
        "A Jurchen chief, strategist and administrator. He had created an organized and highly mobile army. His sons Daisan and Hong Taiji, the latter a future emperor, commanded part of the forces.",
        { h: "Yang Hao" },
        "The Ming general who set up the operation. He sent the four columns, waited for the plan to work and saw his army destroyed. He spent ten years in prison and was executed afterward.",
        { h: "The column commanders" },
        "Du Song, in the west, the largest of the columns; Ma Lin, in the north, with an allied Yehe contingent, commanded by Gintaisi; Liu Ting, in the east, together with the Koreans of General Gang Hong-rip; and Li Rubai, in the south."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The Jin had about 60,000 men. The Ming, between 90,000 and 120,000, according to the estimates, with a contingent of about 13,000 Koreans. By column: Du Song, some 30,000; Ma Lin, close to 10,000, plus the Yehe allies; Liu Ting and the Koreans, some 20,000 in total; Li Rubai, around 40,000. All the numbers are approximate.",
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "A region of mountains and valleys east of Shenyang, with snow still on the passes and the Hun River cutting across the path of the west column. For someone who knows the terrain, this is a place for ambushes.",
        { h: "The battle" },
        { formation: true },
        "The west column, under Du Song, was first. He took Fushun, which was undefended, and went on through the snow to the Hun River. Nurhaci had dams broken upriver. Du crossed with about 10,000 men, and some 20,000 stayed behind. The army was split into two camps, at Sarhu and at Jilin.",
        "Nurhaci attacked the separated camps. The one at Sarhu was taken by surprise and routed. Du Song, Wang Xuan and Zhao Menglin died. According to the accounts, Du was killed by an arrow attributed to Laimbu.",
        "Next, the north column. Ma Lin had fortified three camps, with cannons and trenches. Nurhaci sent scouting horsemen, then an infantry feint, and finally a surprise attack with the banners. Ma's gunners got off only one volley before the Jurchens arrived. Ma fled, and the other two camps fell quickly.",
        "Then, the east column. Liu Ting had already had some small successes. Nurhaci's spies handed him a false letter, saying that Du Song was near Hetu Ala. Liu hurried his march, entering a valley, where Daisan and Hong Taiji ambushed him, at the Abudali pass. Liu was killed. The Korean musketeers, hindered by the wind, fought until their allies surrendered, and General Gang Hong-rip surrendered with the rest of his men.",
        "The south column, under Li Rubai, was ordered to fall back and returned with few losses."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "The loss figures are uncertain. One estimate speaks of about 45,000 Ming casualties, plus some 28,000 horses. It was an enormous defeat for the Ming.",
        "Yang Hao was imprisoned and later executed. Li Rubai was accused of having ties with Nurhaci and killed himself before the trial. Ma Lin was captured and killed at Kaiyuan, a few months later.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "It was the first big step of the Manchu conquest. In the following months, the initiative passed to Nurhaci: he took Kaiyuan, Tieling and finished off the Yehe. In 1621, he took Shenyang and Liaoyang. In 1644, his heirs entered Beijing and founded the Qing dynasty, the last of China.",
        "Nurhaci had fewer men, but used the advantage of fighting at home, of having a more mobile army and of facing the columns one at a time, before they could unite. It is a classic example of beating the stronger by dividing him.",
        "For Korea, the consequences were felt later. Nurhaci asked why the Koreans had helped the Ming, and the Koreans sent a polite answer, but without recognizing his rule. Gang Hong-rip was kept at court for speaking the Jurchen language, and in 1627 encouraged an invasion of Korea. Korea, for its part, changed its tactics, beginning to combine pikemen and musketeers.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The line attributed to Nurhaci, something like 'come by however many roads you like, I will go by only one', is the most famous of the battle. It should be treated as tradition: it appears in popular accounts, and we have no way of knowing whether he said it." },
        { note: "The dates: 14 to 18 April 1619 in the Gregorian calendar. In Chinese sources, the days appear in the lunar calendar, which complicates comparison. The battle began on the first day of the third lunar month." },
        { note: "The numbers: the Ming have 90,000 to 120,000, the Jin 60,000. But these are estimates, and the numbers vary a lot from one source to another." },
        { note: "The name: at the time, Nurhaci's men were called Jurchens. The name 'Manchu' was only adopted in 1635, by his son Hong Taiji." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  breitenfeld: {
    war: "Thirty Years' War: the Swedish phase (1630–1635)",
    hook: "The king of Sweden showed Europe that an army could reorganize itself in the middle of a battle.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "The Thirty Years' War began in 1618, as a quarrel between Catholics and Protestants inside the [[Holy Roman Empire|sacro império]], and spread across all of Europe. By 1630, the Catholics had been winning almost everything: the armies of the emperor and the Catholic League had defeated Denmark, and the emperor decreed, in 1629, the [[Edict|édito]] of Restitution, which ordered the return to the Catholic Church of lands the Protestants had taken.",
        "For the German Protestants, the situation was desperate. It was then that the king of Sweden, Gustavus Adolphus, crossed the Baltic and landed in Pomerania, in 1630, with almost 18,000 men. He was a convinced Protestant, but Sweden also had its own interests in the Baltic, and support from the German princes was slow to come.",
        { h: "The road to Breitenfeld" },
        { timeline: [
          ["1630", "Gustavus Adolphus lands in Pomerania and forces an alliance on the local duke."],
          ["January 1631", "Treaty of Bärwalde: France promises money to Sweden, and the Swedish army reaches about 36,000 men."],
          ["May 1631", "Count Tilly takes and destroys Magdeburg, a Protestant city. It is a warning of what was to come."],
          ["September 1631", "Tilly invades Saxony. The elector of Saxony joins the Swedes, and the two armies march on Leipzig, where Tilly was."],
          ["17 September 1631", "Battle of Breitenfeld."]
        ]},
        "The sack of Magdeburg, in May 1631, showed the Protestant princes what would happen if the emperor won, and pushed several of them to the Swedish side. The elector of Saxony, John George I, also ended up joining the alliance."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "The main players", blocks: [
        { h: "Gustavus Adolphus, 36 years old" },
        "The king of Sweden, a soldier and military reformer. He reduced the size of the infantry units, trained the soldiers to fire by [[volley|salva]] and gave each regiment light cannons. He also trained the cavalry to attack with the sword, instead of standing still firing pistols. The result was an army more flexible than those of his time.",
        { h: "Count Tilly, about 72 years old" },
        "The commander of the Catholic League army, a veteran of many wars. He preferred the traditional formation, in large infantry blocks, the [[tercios|tércio]]. He came from many victories, the most recent of them the capture of Magdeburg.",
        { h: "The other names" },
        "On the Swedish side, Gustaf Horn commanded the left wing, Johan Banér the cavalry, and Lennart Torstensson the artillery. Major General Teuffel and the Scottish colonel Hepburn had the infantry brigades. The Saxons were commanded by the elector John George I and by Hans Georg von Arnim. On Tilly's side, Gottfried zu Pappenheim, impetuous, commanded the cavalry, and Egon von Fürstenberg and the Croat Isolani also commanded horsemen."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The Swedish-Saxon army had about 40,000 men (22,800 Swedes and 17,350 Saxons) and 66 cannons. That of the League and the Empire had about 31,400 (14,700 imperials, 15,700 of the League and 1,000 irregulars) and 27 cannons. There are somewhat larger counts for Tilly's side.",
        "The great difference was organization. The Swedes grouped themselves in [[brigades|brigada]] of three or four battalions, easier to maneuver than the [[tercios|tércio]], and supported by [[regimental guns|artilharia de regimento]]. The light cannons fired much faster than the heavy artillery (tradition says three times as fast).",
        { h: "The terrain and the wind" },
        "The battle was near Breitenfeld, on the outskirts of Leipzig, in open country. Tilly had taken position on the Galgenberg, a hill that Gustavus Adolphus hesitated to attack. The Saxons were on the Swedish left, and Horn commanded the left of the Swedish army. A strong southwest wind blew dust and smoke into the Swedes' eyes, and the king swung the right wing around to take the wind out of his men's faces.",
        { h: "The battle" },
        { formation: true },
        "The battle began around midday, with two hours of cannon fire from both sides. Torstensson's artillery battered the tercios. About 1,000 Swedes, 1,000 Saxons and 2,000 men of the League and the Empire died.",
        "Around 2 in the afternoon, Pappenheim attacked the Swedish right, without orders from Tilly. Musket volleys and Swedish counter-charges repelled him, and he tried again, seven times in all, and even tried to go around the king by the flank.",
        "Around 3 in the afternoon, Tilly launched the main attack. His tercios advanced diagonally, toward the Saxons and the Swedish center-left, but came under artillery fire. Fürstenberg and Isolani fell with cavalry on the center and left of the Saxons. Around 4 in the afternoon, the Saxons fell apart and fled, and the imperial cavalry captured their cannons.",
        "That was when the difference in preparation showed. With the flank opened by the flight of the Saxons, Horn swung his left wing and formed, in about 15 minutes, a new front at a right angle to the old one, using the smoke and dust as cover. The musketeers repelled Fürstenberg's attack. Reinforcements from the second line, among them Hepburn's Scottish brigade, arrived to hold Pappenheim's tercios, and the cannons helped.",
        "Banér's cavalry counterattacked and, after the seventh repulse, pushed Pappenheim's [[cuirassiers|couraceiros]] off the field. Then Gustavus Adolphus led a cavalry charge along the flank, took the cannons of the Galgenberg and turned their muzzles against the imperial infantry.",
        "Between 6 and 7 in the evening, Tilly's army broke. Horn's last charge and the attack of Hepburn's infantry, together with the crossfire of the artillery, destroyed the tercios. Tilly was wounded in the chest and neck, and the cuirassiers of Cronberg and Schönburg took him to Halle. Pappenheim held the Linkelwald wood with a few tercios and withdrew in the dark. The fighting ended around 9 in the evening."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "The League and the Empire lost 7,000 to 8,000 dead and some 6,000 prisoners, besides all their artillery and 120 standards. Many of the dead were senior officers. The Swedes lost about 3,550 men (2,100 infantry and 1,450 cavalry), among them Major General Teuffel. The Saxons lost 2,000 to 3,000, most of them in the bombardment and the flight.",
        "The imperial army split: Tilly withdrew to the south, and Pappenheim went toward the Weser River.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "It was the first big Protestant victory of the war. After it, the allies debated whether to march straight to Vienna or go west, to the Rhine. The Saxons went on to the Habsburg hereditary lands, and the Swedes went southwest. Erfurt and Würzburg fell in the autumn, and Mainz was taken on 11 December 1631. Hanover, Hesse, Brandenburg and Saxony itself lined up against the emperor, and France increased its financial support.",
        "Tilly did not recover: he was wounded again in April 1632 and died. The emperor called Wallenstein back, and in November 1632 he faced Gustavus Adolphus at Lützen, where the king of Sweden was killed.",
        "Militarily, Breitenfeld is remembered as proof that fire, mobility and the combination of arms worked better than large infantry blocks. The defeat led the imperial side to adopt battalions and brigades.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The decision not to march straight to Vienna after the victory is debated to this day. No one can say what would have happened." },
        { note: "The calendar: the battle is dated 17 September 1631, in the Gregorian calendar, which the Catholics used. The Protestants still used the Julian calendar, in which the date was 7 September." },
        { note: "The Saxons: they got a reputation for having fled, but the sources say that many of their 2,000 to 3,000 casualties came from the initial bombardment or from the flight itself, after the imperial cavalry attacked their center and left." }
      ]}
    ]
  }
});

